--TEST--
`Core\Command::completions` refuses a program that was not started from a command line, because a completion script has nothing to register against — ADR 0086 § 6
--RUN--
test
--FILE--
<?nvs
use Core\Test;

final class CompletionsTest {
    // `--RUN--` above is what makes this reachable: a `#[Test]` method runs
    // under `nvs test`, which builds the program's context without a command
    // line, and `nvs_runtime::Ctx::program_name` is empty for exactly that
    // reason — the name a script registers against is a fact about how the
    // process was started, and a run that started no process has none.
    //
    // A served request is the other context with no name, and it is the one
    // this rule exists for: the member says so rather than inventing a name
    // that would produce a script completing something nobody can type.
    #[Test]
    public function itRefusesAProgramWithNoName(): void {
        try {
            string $script = Core\Command::completions(Core\Cli\Shell::Bash);
            echo "a script was written for a program with no name\n";
        } catch (LogicError $refused) {
            echo $refused->message, "\n";
        }
        // `rule:testing/runner-is-strict`: a test whose ledger is empty fails, and the claim
        // here is the refusal itself — the shell it was asked for changes
        // nothing, because the name is missing before a generator is chosen.
        Test::assertThrows(
            fn (): void => { Core\Command::completions(Core\Cli\Shell::Zsh); },
            LogicError::class,
        );
    }
}
--EXPECTF--
  CompletionsTest
`Core\Command::completions` has no name to register a script against: this program was not started from a command line, and a served request is not something a shell completes
    ✓ itRefusesAProgramWithNoName      %f ms

  0 failed, 1 passed, 0 skipped, 0 flaky in %d ms
